WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:14.900
ISO/IEC 27018:2025 is about a specific privacy problem: personally identifiable information processed in public cloud services when the cloud service provider acts as a PII processor.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.320
PII means information that can identify a person, either directly or by being combined with other information.

3
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:31.500
Public cloud means a cloud service available to multiple cloud service customers using shared provider-managed infrastructure, platforms, or applications.

4
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:40.560
The processor role matters because the provider is processing PII on behalf of a customer rather than deciding the primary purposes of that processing for itself.

5
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:56.280
The current edition is titled ISO/IEC 27018:2025, Information security, cybersecurity and privacy protection, Guidelines for protection of personally identifiable information in public clouds acting as PII processors.

6
00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:01.180
The title signals both the privacy focus and the cloud processing boundary.

7
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:14.720
The standard is relevant to large hyperscale providers, specialized software as a service providers, managed platform providers, and smaller cloud services when they process customer PII as processors in a public cloud context.

8
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:25.300
It is also useful to cloud service customers, procurement teams, security architects, and auditors who need a disciplined way to evaluate provider commitments and evidence.

9
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.180
The distinction between security and privacy is central.

10
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:52.080
Confidentiality controls are necessary, but privacy protection also involves purpose limitation, transparency, processing instructions, deletion, return of data, subprocessor oversight, support access, disclosure handling, accountability, and assistance with PII-principal rights where the customer relationship and applicable obligations call for it.

11
00:01:52.180 --> 00:02:05.700
A provider can encrypt data and still create privacy risk if it uses customer PII for unclear secondary purposes, retains derived records without a defined basis, or cannot explain where replicas and support artifacts reside.

12
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:16.540
ISO/IEC 27018 is a guideline standard, not a privacy law, not a universal contract, and not a replacement for ISO/IEC 27001.

13
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:26.000
It supports risk treatment and control implementation, but it does not by itself establish an Information Security Management System, or ISMS.

14
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:33.460
It also does not guarantee that an incident will never happen or that every processing activity satisfies every privacy law.

15
00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:41.160
Legal duties, regulatory duties, customer instructions, and contract terms need their own analysis.

16
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:48.240
The 2025 edition is important because it is aligned with ISO/IEC 27002:2022.

17
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:54.980
That alignment helps organizations connect public cloud PII processing guidance to the modern information security control structure.

18
00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:01.640
Official ISO information also describes a new Annex B that offers extended implementation guidance.

19
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:13.820
Because this course does not rely on an unauthorized full copy of the standard, it teaches the framework and operational themes at a high level rather than pretending to reproduce every control or clause.

20
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:26.020
An engineering view of ISO/IEC 27018 starts with scope, roles, data flows, obligations, risks, controls, evidence, and continual improvement.

21
00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.960
The question is not simply whether a provider can display a badge.

22
00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:44.780
The question is whether the provider and customer understand what PII is processed, why it is processed, who instructs the processing, which systems and people touch it, which safeguards operate, which evidence proves operation, and how changes are reviewed over time.

23
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:52.180
The course therefore treats ISO/IEC 27018 as a way to organize cloud privacy engineering decisions.

24
00:03:52.280 --> 00:04:10.920
A cloud service team should be able to draw the processing boundary, list the PII categories, identify service components, name customer and provider responsibilities, and explain which commitments are implemented in configuration, procedure, contract language, support practice, and monitoring.

25
00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:17.660
A procurement team should be able to evaluate whether a provider's assurance material actually covers the service and region being purchased.

26
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:24.880
An audit or governance team should be able to trace a claim from policy to evidence without inventing details from incomplete public summaries.

27
00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.140
That disciplined trace is the practical starting point for the remaining modules.
